 computing in the 1990s to  current experiments with electronic and  reactive text both we have a new  Institute here at MIT advanced  functional fibers of America huge  consortium public-private thing we've  done shrink-wrapped spacesuits all kinds  of material science explorations in this  area and of course the proliferation a  portable sensing systems that provide  data feedback to monitor health or  improve athletic performance in many  ways so what we want to do ultimately is  to bring this question of the  intersection of the digital and material  back to the body and the position of the  body in space and to ask what it means  today to be human  not so human where is this intersection  and proliferation so I now like to  introduce the panelists and the order in  which they will speak our first  conversation will would we be between  Hussein Shanahan Salah yang excuse me a  fashion designer who as I mentioned for  more than 20 years has used clothing as  a platform to display materials that  change state and transform themselves  his work is characterized by an  adventurous approach to the runway show  and he's been able to use these  performances really as a mechanism a  means to address all kinds of issues  from disembodiment metamorphosis forced  migration all kinds of interesting  social and self representational issues  he will be in conversation with Michelle  Fenimore who is the I know you have the  title yes appending the penny Vinick  curator of textual arts at the Museum of  Fine Arts here in Boston I hope that  many of you were able to see the show  that she curated recently called textile  in which Hussein and others some people  from Media Lab showed their work she is  also the author of  Hollywood before glamour fashion and  American silent film natasha shul is an  associate professor of media culture and  communication at New York University but  for many years was oh sorry my slide  excuse me where's my here we go  sorry here you go there are 